<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=magnetic-resonance-imaging">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Medicine and health</p><h1 class="enc-title">Magnetic resonance imaging</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1973">1973</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as MRI, NMRI, Nuclear magnetic resonance imaging</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) is a medical imaging technique that uses strong magnetic fields, field gradients and radio waves to form pictures of the organs, without <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/x-ray">X-rays</a> or ionising radiation. It applies nuclear magnetic resonance, invented in the 1940s and 1950s to study physical systems. In the early 1970s Carlton Hazlewood, Donald Chang and Raymond Damadian showed that NMR relaxation times could help detect cancer, and in 1973 Paul Lauterbur proposed a magnetic field gradient to make a two-dimensional NMR image. Peter Mansfield and Lauterbur received the 2003 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-prize">Nobel Prize</a> in Physiology or Medicine.</p></div>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>1973</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Stony Brook University</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd>Paul Lauterbur · Raymond Damadian · Peter Mansfield · Carlton Hazlewood · Donald Chang</dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>Raymond Damadian filed a patent in late 1972 for a full-body NMR machine to detect cancer, and built one of the first scanners. The 1973 gradient method of Lauterbur is credited with the first two-dimensional image.</p><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=magnetic-resonance-imaging">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
